PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 and 11–14 have already shown that a thr-proximal counterclockwise origin places azi immediately downstream of oriT and thereby produces the single long expression window required by the data. I now see that the earlier claim of topological equivalence between A and D is not exact. In the clockwise-ton framing the first DNA segment handed over is actually the thr–leu interval, so thr would appear before azi and the observed prolonged azis-only phase would be lost. The minute-number scatter already noted for ton (27 min versus 76–85 min) simply magnifies that ordering problem.  

By contrast, the thr-counterclockwise geometry is fixed on the standard map: thr sits immediately clockwise of azi, transfer proceeds in the opposite direction, and azi is the sole earliest marker recovered. That arrangement matches both the classic HfrH time-of-entry series and the single-marker persistence reported in the experiment.  

Origins near lac (B), pro (C) or str (E) place azi well downstream on either polarity and cannot generate an extended early azis window. The only configuration free of these contradictions remains the thr-proximal counterclockwise donor, option D. The lac-proximal counterclockwise case (B) might be worth a quick check against the raw entry times, but on present evidence it still leaves azi too late to explain the prolonged expression.}

